            <ArticleTitle>Parent-reported prevalence of food allergy in Mexican schoolchildren: A population-based study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>563-570</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0301-0546(16)30061-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.aller.2016.03.003</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Food allergy (FA) prevalence is well documented in developed countries and appears to be increasing, but remains unknown in most Latin American countries. We aimed to evaluate on a population basis the parent-reported prevalence of FA and its clinical characteristics in Mexican schoolchildren.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A validated Spanish version of a structured written questionnaire was administered to parents of schoolchildren aged 5-13 years old from Culiacan, Mexico.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 1049 parents responded to the survey (response rate, 84%). The estimated prevalence rates (95% CI) were: adverse food reactions 10.0% (8.3-11.9), &quot;perceived FA, ever&quot; 5.5% (4.3-7.0), &quot;physician-diagnosed FA, ever&quot; 4.9% (3.7-6.3), &quot;immediate-type FA, ever&quot; 4.4% (3.3-5.8), &quot;immediate-type FA, current&quot; 3.5% (2.6-4.8), and anaphylaxis 1.2% (0.72-2.1). Immediate hypersensitivity reactions were mainly triggered by the consumption of shrimp (1.3%), other shellfish (0.7%), strawberry (0.6%), chocolate (0.5%), and egg (0.4%). Schoolchildren with &quot;immediate-type FA, current&quot; had more atopic dermatitis and allergic rhinitis (p&lt;0.05), but not asthma or drug allergy (p&gt;0.05) than children without FA. All cases of anaphylaxis sought medical attention, but only one child had physician-diagnosed anaphylaxis and was advised to acquire an epinephrine autoinjector.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The prevalence of &quot;immediate-type FA, current&quot; to any food is 3.5% in Mexican schoolchildren. The poor recognition of anaphylaxis and the low frequency of prescription of epinephrine autoinjectors suggest that acute food-induced allergic reactions are not optimally managed in Mexico.</AbstractText>
